## Independent session timers

Each timed game session must store:
- Customer ID.
- Visit ID.
- Game/product ID.
- Resource ID, where applicable.
- Actual start timestamp.
- Scheduled end timestamp.
- Configured grace period.
- Grace deadline.
- Actual logout timestamp.
- Session status.
- Original rate and applicable price.
- Extension history.
- Staff member who started the session.
- Staff member who ended the session.

Calculate scheduled end time from the actual start timestamp plus the selected duration.

Calculate the grace deadline from the scheduled end timestamp plus the configured grace period.

Use server-generated or server-validated timestamps.

Calculate displayed remaining time from stored timestamps. Do not rely on a JavaScript counter as the source of truth.

Multiple sessions for the same customer must run independently.

Starting a second game must not restart the first game's timer.

Prevent duplicate or overlapping assignments of a single-capacity resource. Handle resource allocation inside a safe database transaction or equivalent concurrency-controlled server operation.

## Five-minute grace period

Set the initial grace period to five minutes, configurable by the owner.

At the scheduled end time:
- Change the session state to Due.
- Display an in-app alert asking the receptionist to inform the customer.
- Show the customer, game, resource and expiry time.

After the configured grace period:
- Change the state to Overdue.
- Highlight the elapsed overdue duration.
- Keep the session visible until resolved.

The grace period is an operational buffer. It must not automatically create additional paid minutes, alter the invoice or silently extend the session.

